## Step 1: Understand the concept of projection matrices.
A projection matrix is used to project a vector from one space to another. In this case, we want to project a vector onto the y-axis.

## Step 2: Identify the basis vectors of the y-axis projection.
The basis vectors of a 2D plane projected onto the y-axis have the form <0, y> for any y in the domain.

## Step 3: Use the standard form of a projection matrix.
The general formula for a projection matrix onto a vector <a, b> is ( <a, b>.<a, b> / (| <a, b>|^2 ),  <a, b>.<a, b> / (| <a, b>|^2 ), 0, 0 )

## Step 4: Calculate the projection matrix onto the y-axis.
The basis vector for the y-axis is <0, 1>. First, compute the dot product: <0, 1>.<0, 1> = (0)(0) + (1)(1) = 1. Then, compute the magnitude of this basis vector squared: |<0, 1>|^2 = 0^2 + 1^2 = 1. Now, we get the projection matrix formula components: P(0, x) = P(y, 0) = 1/1 = 1 and P(x, 0) = P(0, y) = 0.

## Step 5: Use the formula to calculate the overall projection matrix P.
P = \mid *[1  0; 0  1] \mid


## Step 6: Write the projection matrix as matrix form in the appropriate format.
